  Line 34:     { topic: 'the basis of electrophoresis', correct: 'It separates molecules according to charge and size in an electric field.', wrongs: ['It separates molecules only by temperature.', 'It works only with oil droplets.', 'It measures pKa directly.'], why: 'Charged species migrate at different rates depending on their net charge, shape, and size.', analogy: 'It is like runners in a race being sorted by how fast their shoes let them move through a field.' },

  Line 35:     { topic: 'the main reason proteins fold into stable shapes', correct: 'They minimize free energy by balancing covalent, electrostatic, hydrogen-bond, van der Waals, and hydrophobic interactions.', wrongs: ['They fold only because of ATP hydrolysis.', 'They form random shapes without any rules.', 'They fold only in the nucleus.'], why: 'Protein folding is driven by physical interactions that favor one low-energy conformation over many alternatives.', analogy: 'It is like arranging furniture to fit a room and keep it stable without crowding it.' },

  Line 36:     { topic: 'the significance of pH in biological systems', correct: 'It affects charge states, enzyme activity, and binding interactions by altering protonation.', wrongs: ['It only affects DNA structure.', 'It has no impact on protein behavior.', 'It only matters outside the cell.'], why: 'Changing pH modifies the protonation of ionizable groups, which can shift catalysis and molecular recognition.', analogy: 'It is like changing the grip strength on a tool by adjusting the handle.' },

  Line 37:     { topic: 'the function of protein side chains', correct: 'They determine chemical reactivity, folding preferences, and binding specificity.', wrongs: ['They only create the peptide backbone.', 'They are irrelevant once a protein is made.', 'They serve as a permanent energy bank.'], why: 'Different side chains vary in charge, size, and polarity, so they interact selectively with partners and the environment.', analogy: 'They are like the different tools in a toolbox, each suited for a different job.' },

  Line 38:     { topic: 'the role of weak interactions in folding', correct: 'They collectively stabilize folding even though each individual interaction is modest.', wrongs: ['They are always impossible in water.', 'They only happen in membranes.', 'They replace the need for covalent bonds.'], why: 'Many weak interactions add up to a stable structure when arranged in the right geometry.', analogy: 'It is like many small hands holding a tent steady in a breeze.' },

  Line 39:     { topic: 'the arrangement of nonpolar residues in a protein interior', correct: 'They tend to be buried away from water because hydrophobic effects favor packing them together.', wrongs: ['They are spread across the protein surface to make it more soluble.', 'They are always charged to stay inside the protein.', 'They are destroyed during translation.'], why: 'Keeping nonpolar groups away from water reduces unfavorable ordering of water molecules and stabilizes the folded state.', analogy: 'It is like a crowd moving under cover during a storm instead of standing in the rain.' },

  Line 40:     { topic: 'the cell’s use of compartments', correct: 'They separate incompatible processes and help create gradients and local environments.', wrongs: ['They make every reaction happen at the same rate.', 'They are only decorative structures.', 'They eliminate the need for membranes.'], why: 'Compartmentation allows the cell to regulate chemistry by concentrating reagents and minimizing cross-talk.', analogy: 'It is like dividing a kitchen into prep, cooking, and cleaning stations.' },

  Line 41:     { topic: 'the significance of the Henderson–Hasselbalch equation', correct: 'It relates pH, pKa, and the ratio of conjugate base to acid in a buffer.', wrongs: ['It only applies to enzymes.', 'It tells exactly how fast a buffer works.', 'It describes DNA replication.'], why: 'The equation lets us predict pH from buffer composition, which is essential in biological systems and analytical chemistry.', analogy: 'It is like a recipe formula telling you how much of each ingredient sets the final flavor.' },

  Line 42:     { topic: 'the importance of the amino acid backbone', correct: 'It provides a repeating scaffold while the side chains provide diversity and function.', wrongs: ['It is irrelevant once translation ends.', 'It consists only of phosphate and sugar.', 'It is identical in all proteins regardless of sequence.'], why: 'The backbone is conserved while side chains create the unique chemistry of each protein.', analogy: 'It is like the same frame of a bus with different seats and decorations for different riders.' },

  Line 43:     { topic: 'the relationship between amino acid sequence and structure', correct: 'Sequence determines the possible interactions that guide folding and thus the final structure.', wrongs: ['Structure is independent of sequence.', 'Sequence only matters in DNA.', 'Sequence is irrelevant in enzymes.'], why: 'The order of side chains sets up hydrogen bonding, ionic interactions, and hydrophobic packing during folding.', analogy: 'It is like a set of instructions telling you how to arrange pieces in a puzzle.' },

  Line 44:     { topic: 'the meaning of a catalytic active site', correct: 'It is the region of an enzyme where substrate binding and chemical transformation occur.', wrongs: ['It is the entire enzyme outside the substrate-binding region.', 'It is a storage site for ATP.', 'It is found only in cell membranes.'], why: 'The active site provides a microenvironment and orientation that speed chemical reactions.', analogy: 'It is like the workbench where a craftsman shapes parts into a finished product.' },

  Line 45:     { topic: 'the difference between a substrate and a product', correct: 'A substrate is the starting material that enters a reaction, whereas a product is the final species formed.', wrongs: ['A product is the enzyme that catalyzes the reaction.', 'A substrate is the enzyme’s end state.', 'They are identical in every metabolic pathway.'], why: 'Reaction diagrams track conversion from substrate to product as bonds change and energy is released or absorbed.', analogy: 'It is like raw ingredients versus the finished meal after cooking.' },

  Line 46:     { topic: 'the concept of a transition state', correct: 'It is the highest-energy, fleeting arrangement of atoms along the reaction pathway.', wrongs: ['It is the final product of the reaction.', 'It is the same as the substrate before binding.', 'It always has lower energy than the product.'], why: 'Enzymes lower the activation barrier by stabilizing the transition state, making the reaction proceed faster.', analogy: 'It is like the crest of a hill before a car goes downhill.' },

  Line 47:     { topic: 'the reason enzymes are not consumed in reactions', correct: 'They are regenerated after catalysis and can participate in many cycles.', wrongs: ['They are permanently used up like a reactant.', 'They mutate into products each time.', 'They turn into ATP.'], why: 'Catalysis changes the reaction pathway, not the enzyme itself, so the catalyst is returned to its original state.', analogy: 'It is like a race official who helps every runner without becoming a runner themselves.' },

  Line 48:     { topic: 'the impact of a competitive inhibitor', correct: 'It competes with the substrate for the active site and can reduce the rate of catalysis.', wrongs: ['It permanently destroys the enzyme.', 'It alters the DNA coding for the enzyme.', 'It removes the product from the cell.'], why: 'Competitive inhibitors occupy the active site reversibly, limiting access to the substrate.', analogy: 'It is like someone standing in front of the door and blocking entry to the room.' },

  Line 49:     { topic: 'the key idea of the Michaelis constant (Km)', correct: 'It reflects the substrate concentration at which the reaction rate is half-maximal.', wrongs: ['It is the maximum velocity of the reaction.', 'It is always zero for all enzymes.', 'It measures pH directly.'], why: 'Km estimates the affinity of an enzyme for its substrate, with lower Km usually suggesting tighter binding.', analogy: 'It is like the amount of traffic needed to reach half the road’s maximum flow.' },

  Line 50:     { topic: 'the role of coenzymes in metabolism', correct: 'They assist enzymes by carrying chemical groups or electrons during reactions.', wrongs: ['They are permanent parts of DNA.', 'They make all enzymes work without substrate.', 'They are only found in the membrane.'], why: 'Coenzymes such as NAD+, FAD, and CoA shuttle electrons or acyl groups between metabolic steps.' , analogy: 'They are like specialized helpers taking supplies from one station to another.' }

  Line 53:     { topic: 'water’s cohesive behavior', correct: 'Water molecules stick together mainly through hydrogen bonds.', wrongs: ['They are linked by peptide bonds.', 'They share permanent covalent bonds with each other.', 'They repel each other in all conditions.'], why: 'The polar O–H bonds allow partial charges that attract neighboring water molecules through hydrogen bonds.', analogy: 'It is like a line of friends holding hands in a crowd.' },

  Line 54:     { topic: 'the pH of a solution', correct: 'It measures the concentration of hydrogen ions on a logarithmic scale.', wrongs: ['It measures temperature directly.', 'It is the concentration of water in solution.', 'It is the same as buffer capacity.'], why: 'Each pH unit represents a tenfold change in hydrogen ion concentration.', analogy: 'It is like a Richter scale for acidity, where each step is a jump in magnitude.' },

  Line 55:     { topic: 'a solution at pH 3 compared with pH 5', correct: 'It is 100 times more acidic.', wrongs: ['It is 2 times more acidic.', 'It is 10 times more acidic.', 'It is 1,000 times more acidic.'], why: 'The pH scale is logarithmic, so a two-unit difference equals a 10² change.', analogy: 'It is like a 100-fold jump rather than a small step change.' },

  Line 56:     { topic: 'the high heat of vaporization of water', correct: 'It means a lot of heat is required to convert liquid water to vapor.', wrongs: ['It means water freezes easily.', 'It means water dissolves only in oil.', 'It means water lacks hydrogen bonds.'], why: 'Breaking intermolecular attractions in liquid water requires substantial energy, which is why evaporative cooling works.', analogy: 'It is like a large amount of energy needed to pry a crowded group apart.' },

  Line 57:     { topic: 'a hydrophobic molecule in water', correct: 'It tends to cluster away from water because it cannot form favorable interactions with it.', wrongs: ['It dissolves completely and spreads out.', 'It forms a strong salt bridge with water.', 'It becomes positively charged.'], why: 'Nonpolar molecules disrupt water’s hydrogen-bond network and are therefore driven together.', analogy: 'It is like oil beads avoiding a puddle instead of mixing with it.' },

  Line 58:     { topic: 'the conjugate base of carbonic acid after one proton is removed', correct: 'It is bicarbonate, HCO3−.', wrongs: ['It is carbonate, CO3^2−.', 'It is carbon dioxide, CO2.', 'It is hydroxide, OH−.'], why: 'Removing one proton from H2CO3 leaves HCO3−, the conjugate base in the blood buffer system.', analogy: 'It is like handing off one item from a two-item pair and leaving the remainder behind.' },

  Line 59:     { topic: 'the acidity of a solution as pH decreases', correct: 'Its proton concentration increases and its hydroxide concentration decreases.', wrongs: ['Proton concentration decreases.', 'Both concentration terms decrease equally.', 'It becomes less acidic because water is removed.'], why: 'Acidity reflects how much H+ is present relative to OH−.', analogy: 'It is like a crowded room with more people on one side than the other.' },

  Line 60:     { topic: 'the amphipathic nature of phospholipids', correct: 'They have both hydrophilic heads and hydrophobic tails, which drive bilayer formation.', wrongs: ['They are fully nonpolar.', 'They are fully charged and never face water.', 'They are made only of proteins.'], why: 'Their dual properties let heads face water and tails avoid it, resulting in a bilayer.', analogy: 'It is like a two-sided sandwich with one side craving water and the other avoiding it.' },

  Line 61:     { topic: 'water’s role in acid-base chemistry', correct: 'It can act as both a proton donor and a proton acceptor.', wrongs: ['It is always a strong acid.', 'It can never participate in proton transfer.', 'It always acts as a buffer by itself.'], why: 'Water is amphoteric and participates in autoionization and proton transfer.', analogy: 'It is like a person who can both give and receive tools during a task.' },

  Line 62:     { topic: 'the biological advantage of high water content', correct: 'It allows a stable solvent environment for diffusion, transport, and biochemical reactions.', wrongs: ['It stops all reactions in the cell.', 'It makes proteins insoluble by default.', 'It eliminates the need for membranes.'], why: 'Water supports ionic interactions and reaction kinetics while helping organize macromolecules.', analogy: 'It is like a busy city’s water supply enabling transport and trade.' },

  Line 63:     { topic: 'the effect of adding strong acid to a weakly buffered solution', correct: 'The pH drops, but not as dramatically as it would without the buffer.', wrongs: ['The pH stays exactly constant forever.', 'The pH immediately becomes 7.', 'The buffer disappears without any consequence.'], why: 'Buffers absorb some added H+ by converting conjugate base to acid, limiting the pH change.', analogy: 'It is like a sponge soaking up a spill without preventing all movement.' },

  Line 64:     { topic: 'the relationship between pH and pKa in a buffer', correct: 'When pH equals pKa, the acid and conjugate base are present in equal amounts.', wrongs: ['pH equals pKa only at 0.', 'The ratio is always 1:10.', 'The acid disappears completely.'], why: 'This condition gives the buffer maximum capacity for neutralizing added acid or base within one pH unit.', analogy: 'It is like a balance point where two sides are equally loaded.' },

  Line 65:     { topic: 'the reason ice floats on liquid water', correct: 'Hydrogen bonding arranges water molecules in an open crystalline lattice that is less dense.', wrongs: ['Ice is denser because it contains more atoms.', 'Water molecules stop moving in liquid water.', 'Ice forms only in the presence of salt.'], why: 'The open lattice causes ice to occupy more volume for the same mass, lowering density.', analogy: 'It is like a honeycomb structure that takes up more space than a compact block.' },

  Line 66:     { topic: 'the role of water in hydrolysis', correct: 'It is used to split a bond, often releasing smaller molecules.', wrongs: ['It is never involved in cleavage reactions.', 'It removes all charge from the molecule.', 'It converts proteins into DNA.'], why: 'Hydrolysis adds water across a bond and is a major path for digestion and turnover.', analogy: 'It is like using water to weaken and separate two glued pieces.' },

  Line 67:     { topic: 'the reason salts dissolve in water', correct: 'Water stabilizes ions by orienting its partial charges toward the charged species.', wrongs: ['Water only dissolves nonpolar molecules.', 'Salt does not interact with water because it is neutral.', 'Salt dissolves because it repels all solvent.'], why: 'Ion-dipole interactions lower the energy of separated ions in solution.', analogy: 'It is like a crowd arranging itself around a moving object to keep it from crashing.' },

  Line 68:     { topic: 'the term osmosis', correct: 'It is the movement of water across a selectively permeable membrane toward a region of higher effective solute concentration.', wrongs: ['It is the diffusion of all solutes through any barrier.', 'It is ATP transport through a channel.', 'It is movement of proteins in the nucleus.'], why: 'Water moves to balance osmotic pressure and reduce free energy differences across the membrane.', analogy: 'It is like water seeking the fuller side of a partition to even out the pressure.' },

  Line 69:     { topic: 'the effect of a hypertonic solution on a cell', correct: 'It can cause water to leave the cell, shrinking it.', wrongs: ['It makes the cell swell and burst by default.', 'It prevents water movement entirely.', 'It always raises pH.'], why: 'The external solute concentration is greater, drawing water out by osmosis.', analogy: 'It is like a sponge drying out when the surrounding air is saltier than the sponge itself.' },

  Line 70:     { topic: 'a weakly acidic solution', correct: 'It has a pH below 7 but is not fully dissociated.', wrongs: ['It has a pH above 7.', 'It contains no acidity at all.', 'It must be a buffer.'], why: 'Weak acids contribute H+ incompletely, and their pH depends on both concentration and dissociation.', analogy: 'It is like a tap that drips steadily rather than opening fully.' },

  Line 71:     { topic: 'the meaning of electronegativity in water', correct: 'It describes oxygen’s stronger pull on shared electrons than hydrogen’s.', wrongs: ['It means water has no partial charges.', 'It describes only salt ions.', 'It means the molecule has no O–H bonds.'], why: 'Unequal electron sharing creates dipoles that enable hydrogen bonding and solvation.', analogy: 'It is like one teammate pulling harder on a rope than the other.' },

  Line 72:     { topic: 'the role of water in the hydrophobic effect', correct: 'Water reorganizes around nonpolar surfaces, which drives nonpolar molecules to aggregate.', wrongs: ['Water directly binds nonpolar molecules with strong covalent bonds.', 'Water ignores nonpolar molecules completely.', 'Water is hydrophobic by itself.'], why: 'The ordering of water around nonpolar regions is unfavorable, so molecules cluster to reduce that ordering.', analogy: 'It is like a crowded hallway being less stressful when the people stand together instead of spread out.' },

  Line 73:     { topic: 'the property called surface tension', correct: 'It is the cohesive force at the surface of water that resists stretching.', wrongs: ['It is the same as pH.', 'It only occurs in proteins.', 'It measures the speed of evaporation.'], why: 'Hydrogen bonding makes water molecules at the interface pull more strongly inward than outward.', analogy: 'It is like a tight skin across the top of a liquid layer.' },

  Line 74:     { topic: 'the contribution of water to enzyme function', correct: 'It helps solvate substrates and products and often provides the medium for proton transfer.', wrongs: ['It keeps all enzymes permanently rigid.', 'It prevents substrate binding by design.', 'It makes all enzymes nonpolar.'], why: 'Water supports flexibility, access to active sites, and proton movement during catalysis.', analogy: 'It is like the stage on which the actors move and interact.' },

  Line 75:     { topic: 'the formula for pH', correct: 'pH = −log10[H+].', wrongs: ['pH = log10[H+].', 'pH = [H+] × 10.', 'pH = OH− concentration.'], why: 'The logarithmic scale compresses a wide range of hydrogen ion concentrations into a manageable 0–14 range.', analogy: 'It is like using a logarithm to measure a huge range without losing the pattern.' },

  Line 76:     { topic: 'the distinction between acid strength and concentration', correct: 'A strong acid can be dilute, and a weak acid can be concentrated; strength and concentration are separate concepts.', wrongs: ['A dilute acid is always weak.', 'Concentration and strength are the same thing.', 'Only strong acids can lower pH.'], why: 'Strength describes dissociation, while concentration describes how much acid is present.', analogy: 'It is like knowing how easily someone gives away money versus how much money they have.' },

  Line 77:     { topic: 'the main driving force behind micelle formation', correct: 'It is the hydrophobic effect plus the favorable arrangement of amphipathic molecules.', wrongs: ['It is the formation of peptide bonds.', 'It is ATP hydrolysis by itself.', 'It occurs only in DNA.'], why: 'Amphipathic molecules pack so that hydrophobic tails hide while polar head groups face water.', analogy: 'It is like people organizing around a shared shelter to keep the rain away.' },

  Line 78:     { topic: 'the effect of temperature on water density', correct: 'Liquid water is most dense near 4 °C, while ice is less dense than liquid water.', wrongs: ['Water density is identical at all temperatures.', 'Ice is always denser than liquid water.', 'Temperature changes do not affect hydrogen bonds.'], why: 'Hydrogen bonding and molecular motion change spacing and packing as temperature changes.', analogy: 'It is like a crowd packing tighter in one season and spreading out in another.' },

  Line 79:     { topic: 'the role of water in protein folding', correct: 'It helps drive hydrophobic collapse and stabilizes polar interactions on the protein surface.', wrongs: ['It prevents all folding by competing with side chains.', 'It is irrelevant because proteins are entirely internal.', 'It makes all proteins denature instantly.'], why: 'Water shapes the energetic landscape of folding through solvation and the hydrophobic effect.', analogy: 'It is like the environment that keeps puzzle pieces in the places where they fit best.' },

  Line 80:     { topic: 'the benefit of buffers in blood', correct: 'They help maintain pH near the physiological range for enzymes and transport proteins.', wrongs: ['They increase oxygen pressure in the blood.', 'They remove all carbon dioxide instantly.', 'They replace the need for kidneys.'], why: 'Stable pH preserves protein function and enzyme kinetics under changing metabolic loads.', analogy: 'It is like keeping a room at a comfortable temperature despite a heat source turning on.' },

  Line 81:     { topic: 'the reason a nonpolar solute is poorly soluble in water', correct: 'Water would need to order itself around the solute, which is energetically unfavorable.', wrongs: ['It is poorly soluble because all molecules are weakly acidic.', 'It must be positively charged to be insoluble.', 'It becomes a buffer in water.'], why: 'Hydrophobic surfaces disrupt the hydrogen-bond network and are therefore not favored in solution.', analogy: 'It is like trying to sit in a tight crowd without creating a gap in the arrangement.' },

  Line 82:     { topic: 'the difference between diffusion and osmosis', correct: 'Diffusion is movement of any solute or molecule down a gradient, while osmosis specifically refers to water movement across a membrane.', wrongs: ['They are the same process in every case.', 'Osmosis applies to all solutes.', 'Diffusion only happens through ATP-driven pumps.'], why: 'The membrane and the solute identity determine whether the process is osmosis or a general diffusive movement.', analogy: 'It is like a general movement of people through a station versus water specifically moving through one gate.' },

  Line 83:     { topic: 'the significance of water as a solvent in cells', correct: 'It allows ions, metabolites, and macromolecules to move and react in a highly organized environment.', wrongs: ['It prevents all molecular interactions.', 'It is simply a structural filler with no role in chemistry.', 'It is only present in the extracellular space.'], why: 'The solvent environment supports selective interactions and biochemical equilibria essential for life.', analogy: 'It is like a highway system that moves material to where work gets done.' },

  Line 84:     { topic: 'the effect of adding base to a buffer solution', correct: 'The weak acid donates protons to neutralize the added OH− and the pH changes less than it would otherwise.', wrongs: ['The buffer always becomes strongly acidic.', 'The weak acid gains protons and drives pH down.', 'The solution becomes nonpolar.'], why: 'Buffers consume added acid or base through their conjugate acid-base pairs.', analogy: 'It is like a paid worker absorbing shocks before they reach the rest of the system.' },

  Line 85:     { topic: 'the pH at which a buffer is most effective', correct: 'It is closest to the pKa of the buffering acid-base pair.', wrongs: ['It is always at pH 7.', 'It is always one unit above pKa.', 'It is determined only by temperature.'], why: 'The buffer works best when acid and conjugate base are present in comparable amounts.', analogy: 'It is like a team performing best when both roles are evenly staffed.' }


